  <article>
    <title>Animorphs</title>

    <section>
      <title>Isomorphism</title>

      <p>This is an important fact that we have used implicitly in class that I want you to work through explicitly as an exercise:</p>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>S \subseteq V</m> be a linearly independent subset of a vector space. Then <m>S</m> is a basis for <m>\text{span}(S)</m>.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <proof>
        <p><!-- Intentionally empty in the LaTeX source. --></p>
      </proof>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>T: U \to V</m> be a linear transformation. Let <m>B</m> be a basis for <m>U</m>. Suppose that <m>\ker(T) = \{\vec{0}\}</m>. Show that <m>T(B) = \{T(\vec{v}): \vec{v} \in B\}</m> is a basis for <m>\text{Im}(T)</m>.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <proof>
        <p><!-- Intentionally empty in the LaTeX source. --></p>
      </proof>

      <p>We call a linear transformation an <term>isomorphism</term> if it is also a bijection. That is, if it is both one-to-one and onto.</p>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>U</m> and <m>V</m> be vector spaces of the same dimension. Then let <m>T: U \to V</m>. Show that if <m>\text{rank}(T) = \text{dim}(U)</m>, then <m>T</m> is a linear isomorphism.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <proof>
        <p><!-- Intentionally empty in the LaTeX source. --></p>
      </proof>
    </section>

    <section>
      <title>Unexpected Values</title>

      <!--
      Commented out in the LaTeX source:
      An investors puts money into three stocks with expected returns on the first of a hundred dollars, the second of two hundred dollars, and the third of a loss of one hundred and fifty dollars. How much should they expect their overall on return on investment to be?
      -->

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>What is the expected number of rolls of a six-sided die until roll your first <m>6</m>?</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <proof>
        <p><!-- Intentionally empty in the LaTeX source. --></p>
      </proof>

      <exercise>
        <title>Expected profit from repeated investor pitches</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>An entrepreneur repeatedly pitches a business idea to potential investors. Each pitch is successful with probability <m>1/5</m> independently of all previous pitches. The entrepreneur stops pitching as soon as the first successful pitch occurs. Each pitch costs $200 to prepare and deliver. If a pitch is successful, the entrepreneur receives $8,000 in funding. Let <m>N</m> be the random variable denote the total number of pitches made, including the successful pitch.</p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>Find <m>\mathbb{E}[N]</m>.</p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>Let <m>Y</m> be the random variable denoting the entrepreneur's net profit after the first successful pitch. Express <m>Y</m> in terms of <m>N</m>.</p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>Compute <m>\mathbb{E}[Y]</m>.</p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>Suppose instead that each pitch costs $1,000. Compute the entrepreneur's expected net profit.</p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>More generally, suppose each pitch costs $<m>c</m>. Find the value of <m>c</m> for which the entrepreneur's expected net profit is exactly zero.</p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <proof>
        <p><!-- Intentionally empty in the LaTeX source. --></p>
      </proof>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>Subdivide a circle of radius <m>R</m> centered at <m>(0,0)</m> into cells. Suppose that expected length of the intersection of any line through <m>(0,0)</m> with any give cell is at least <m>L</m>. Show that the expected total number of cells intersected by a ray emanating from <m>(0,0)</m> is at most <m>2R/L</m>.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <proof>
        <p><!-- Intentionally empty in the LaTeX source. --></p>
      </proof>
    </section>
  </article>
